              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "When you buy \"pure\" gold (24-karat gold or \"fine gold\"), you will notice that it is labeled with a purity of **99.9%** (999 fine) or **99.99%** (999.9 fine). It is never labeled as **100% pure** because reaching absolute, perfect purity is physically, chemically, and practically impossible. \n\nThe reasons why gold's purity maxes out just short of 100% include:\n\n### 1. The Limitations of Refining Chemistry\nIn nature, gold is always found mixed with other elements like silver, copper, iron, platinum, and silica. To purify it, refineries use intense chemical and metallurgical processes:\n* **The Miller Process:** Uses chlorine gas to extract impurities, bringing the gold\u2019s purity up to about 99.5%.\n* **The Wohlwill Process:** An electrolytic process that dissolves the gold and plates it back onto a cathode, refining it further to 99.99%.\n\nHowever, chemical reactions and electrolytic processes eventually reach a state of chemical equilibrium. At a microscopic level, it is impossible to force every single foreign atom to separate from the gold atoms. Some trace metals will always remain trapped in the gold's atomic structure.\n\n### 2. Immediate Environmental Contamination\nEven if a refinery could somehow isolate 100% pure gold in a vacuum, the moment that gold is handled, the purity drops. \n* As soon as gold is melted, poured into a mold, or touched by tools, it picks up microscopic contaminants. \n* It absorbs trace elements from the crucible it is melted in, the air, dust, and even the machinery used to stamp it. \n\n### 3. Limits of Scientific Measurement (Assaying)\nFrom a scientific perspective, you cannot prove a absolute negative. Assaying equipment (such as X-ray fluorescence spectrometers or mass spectrometers) measures the presence of elements down to parts-per-million (ppm) or parts-per-billion (ppb). \nBecause these machines have physical detection limits, a laboratory can never legally or scientifically certify a metal as \"100.00% pure.\" They can only certify that the impurities are below the machine's threshold of detection. Therefore, they label the gold with the highest level of certainty they can prove\u2014such as 99.9% or 99.99%.\n\n### 4. Diminishing Returns and Skyrocketing Costs\nWhile \"four nines\" (99.99%) is the standard for investment bullion, some mints have managed to produce \"six nines\" (99.9999%) pure gold for special commemorative coins. However, the cost of refining gold to this extreme level is astronomical. \n\nBecause a 99.9% pure gold bar behaves, looks, and lasts exactly the same as a hypothetically \"100% pure\" gold bar, there is no practical or financial reason to spend millions of dollars trying to filter out the final microscopic trace elements. \n\n### What is the remaining 0.1%?\nThe tiny fraction of a percent that isn't gold usually consists of harmless trace elements that were either native to the original gold ore or introduced during refining. This typically includes microscopic amounts of **silver, copper, nickel, iron, platinum, or lead**.",
